Assuming that $T \geq \ln(e^2N)$, \Cref{alg:q-ftrl-doubling} run with input $J \in (0,N]$ satisfies 
    \begin{multline*}
        R_T \leq 38 e \sqrt{\brb{\avgc \lor J} T \ln\lrb{\frac{e^2 N}{\avgc \lor J \lor 1}} } +  \log_2\lrb{\frac{\avgc}{J}}_+ \\+ \frac{18 e}{5} \log_2\lrb{\frac{4\lrb{\brb{JT \lor \avgc T} \land \ln(e^2N)}}{JT}}_+ \ln\brb{e^2 N} + 1 \,.
    \end{multline*}

In particular, setting $J = \ln(e^2N)/T$ yields that
    \begin{align*}
        R_T \leq 38 e \sqrt{\avgc T \ln\lrb{\frac{e^2 N}{\avgc \lor 1}} } +  \log_2\lrb{\frac{\avgc T}{\ln(e^2N)}}_+ + 46 e \ln\brb{e^2 N} + 1 \,.
    \end{align*}
